Title: The Big Account
Author: Audit Fixture

/*
PROVENANCE. Original work written for this repository as a test fixture, 2026-09-04,
during the advice-quality audit (docs/scoring/ADVICE_RULE_FIXES_2026-09-04.md). Not
derived from any published screenplay. Dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0.

ROLE. The POSITIVE fixture of a matched pair. Deliberately badly made at 10 scenes /
~1,053 words, matched to excellent.fountain on scene count and length: every character
states their feeling outright, the protagonist is passive throughout, scene 2 repeats
scene 1's conversation, nothing turns, and the problem resolves offstage.

Written in ORDINARY BAD ENGLISH on purpose. The calibration corpus's troubled band is
authored in the exact phrasings dialogue.ts and originality.ts already flag ("I feel so
X, honestly", "Trust me on this"), so band-ordering tests there confirm the rules
against text written from the rules. This fixture trips none of those set phrases, which
is why it went undetected: it produced ZERO ON_THE_NOSE findings until the density gate
moved script-wide and the filler slot widened past a five-adverb whitelist.

Lines this fixture exists to protect:
  * "I am worried about the big presentation today, Karen."
  * "Yes, I am still worried about the presentation."   (`still` -- the whitelist miss)
  * "I am very relieved."   /   "I am glad it all worked out."
Do not "improve" any of those lines; each is load-bearing for a specific regression.
*/

INT. OFFICE - MORNING

A big modern office with many desks. DAN MERCER, 31, sits at his desk looking worried. His coworker KAREN LOWE, 40s, walks up to him holding a coffee.

KAREN
Good morning, Dan. You look worried about the big presentation today.

DAN
I am worried about the big presentation today, Karen. If we lose the Halloran account, the company could be in serious trouble and I could lose my job.

KAREN
That is true. The presentation is at four o'clock this afternoon. That gives us plenty of time to get ready.

DAN
I hope so. I have been working at this company for nine years and I have never felt this nervous.

KAREN
You will be fine, Dan. You always are.

INT. OFFICE - BREAK ROOM - MORNING

Dan pours a cup of coffee sadly. Karen comes in behind him.

KAREN
Are you still worried about the presentation?

DAN
Yes, I am still worried about the presentation. I think we might lose the Halloran account.

KAREN
Try not to worry so much. Worrying does not help anybody.

DAN
You are probably right. But I cannot help it. I am a worrier. My father was a worrier too.

KAREN
Family is complicated.

DAN
Yes. Family is complicated.

INT. OFFICE - MR. HALL'S OFFICE - MORNING

MR. HALL, 60s, the boss, sits behind a large desk. Dan stands in front of it nervously.

MR. HALL
Dan, I called you in here because I want to talk to you about the Halloran account. It is the most important account this company has ever had.

DAN
I understand, sir.

MR. HALL
If we lose it, we will have to make cuts. I do not want to make cuts. Nobody wants to make cuts.

DAN
I will do my best, sir.

MR. HALL
That is all I ask. Do your best.

Dan nods and leaves the office quietly.

INT. OFFICE - DAN'S DESK - MIDDAY

Dan sits at his desk and stares at his computer screen. He opens the presentation file and looks at the slides. There are twelve slides. He reads all of them.

Karen walks by again.

KAREN
How are the slides?

DAN
The slides are fine. I think the slides are good.

KAREN
Good.

DAN
I added a chart to slide six. It shows the revenue over the last three years.

KAREN
A chart is a good idea. People like charts.

DAN
I hope Ms. Halloran likes charts. I have never met her before today.

KAREN
She is supposed to be very direct. That is what everyone says about her.

DAN
Direct is fine. I would rather have direct than the other thing.

KAREN
I agree with you about that.

INT. OFFICE - CONFERENCE ROOM - AFTERNOON

Dan practices the presentation alone in the empty conference room. He clicks through the slides one at a time and says the words out loud.

DAN
Thank you all for coming. Halloran Industries has been a leader in its field for thirty years. We believe our proposal will help you grow.

He stops and shakes his head.

DAN (CONT'D)
That was not very good. I need to practice more.

He starts again from the beginning.

INT. OFFICE - HALLWAY - AFTERNOON

Karen finds Dan in the hallway. He looks tired.

KAREN
There you are. I have been looking for you everywhere. It is almost time.

DAN
I know. I am not ready.

KAREN
Nobody is ever ready. That is just how it is.

DAN
I suppose that is true.

KAREN
Come on. Let us go.

DAN
Do you think Mr. Hall is going to be in the room?

KAREN
He said he would be. He wants to show his support for the team.

DAN
That will make me more nervous, honestly.

KAREN
Try to think of it as a good thing. He believes in you.

DAN
I will try to think of it that way. I do not know if it will work.

KAREN
It is worth a try either way.

They walk down the hallway together.

INT. OFFICE - CONFERENCE ROOM - AFTERNOON

The room is full now. MS. HALLORAN, 50s, sits at the head of the table with two ASSISTANTS. Mr. Hall sits beside her. Dan stands at the front and begins the presentation.

DAN
Thank you all for coming. Halloran Industries has been a leader in its field for thirty years. We believe our proposal will help you grow.

MS. HALLORAN
Go on.

DAN
Our team has prepared a full plan covering the first three quarters. We are confident in it.

Ms. Halloran writes something on her notepad. Dan keeps going through the slides. The presentation takes forty minutes.

INT. OFFICE - CONFERENCE ROOM - LATER

The presentation is over. Everyone is quiet. Ms. Halloran closes her notepad.

MS. HALLORAN
That was a very thorough presentation. I appreciate the effort your team put into it.

MR. HALL
We appreciate you giving us the opportunity.

MS. HALLORAN
We will be in touch soon with our decision.

She stands up and shakes hands with everybody. Then she leaves with her assistants.

INT. OFFICE - DAN'S DESK - EVENING

Dan sits at his desk. The office is mostly empty. Karen comes over with her coat on.

KAREN
You did a good job in there.

DAN
Thank you. I still do not know if we got the account.

KAREN
We will find out eventually. There is nothing more we can do tonight.

DAN
That is true. There is nothing more we can do tonight.

KAREN
Go home, Dan. Get some sleep.

DAN
I will. Good night, Karen.

KAREN
Good night, Dan.

INT. OFFICE - MORNING

The next morning. Dan comes in and sits at his desk. Mr. Hall walks over with a piece of paper in his hand.

MR. HALL
Dan, I have news about the Halloran account.

DAN
What is the news, sir?

MR. HALL
They have decided to go with us. We got the account.

DAN
That is wonderful news, sir. I am very relieved.

MR. HALL
You should be proud of yourself, Dan. You did well.

DAN
Thank you, sir. I am glad it all worked out.

Mr. Hall walks away. Dan smiles and goes back to work. Karen looks over at him and smiles too.
